  "result": "Default `[]` is evaluated once, when `def` runs. Every call that omits `items` gets that same list object, so `append` mutates it.\n\n- `add(1)`: appends 1 to shared list -> `[1]`\n- `add(2)`: appends 2 to same list -> `[1, 2]`\n\nFix: use `None` as sentinel, make new list per call.\n\n```python\ndef add(x, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\n```\n\nNow `add(1)` -> `[1]`, `add(2)` -> `[2]`.",
